{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_29","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"apply_patch() { git apply --no-index --whitespace=error -; }\npython - <<'PY' | apply_patch\nimport json\n\nstrategy = {\n    'status': 'ready',\n    'selected_prompt_family': 'paper_standalone',\n    'recommended_title': 'Width and Delayed Generalization in Finite-Horizon Modular Addition',\n    'central_question': 'How does hidden width relate to the separation between training-set fitting and held-out generalization in a fixed modular-addition network under finite training horizons and matched regularization controls?',\n    'central_thesis': 'The retained comparisons show earlier fitting at larger widths without monotone short-horizon generalization, while two longer-horizon width-128 delayed events remain conditional observations rather than qualified evidence of a size law or a decay-clock mechanism.',\n    'narrative_rationale': 'A conditional empirical study is supported by paired width, seed, and arm records, unlike a mechanistic explanation or a search-success story. The studied configuration has no sustained generalization onset in the short-horizon records, but two retained width-128 final records do have delayed onsets. Three final width-32 records remain censored and all final width-256 records are absent. Final evaluation completed but did not qualify. These facts support a coherent account of what this experiment establishes and leaves unidentified, not a universal claim about width, a causal horizon comparison, or superiority. Readiness means a supportable writing plan, not scientific qualification or a prediction of venue acceptance.',\n    'prior_paper_story_status': 'rejected',\n    'edit_framing_decision': 'No edit mode, prior manuscript, staged replacement implementation, or resolved edit-context file is present. The status value denotes rejection of an inherited untested mechanism story, not rejection of an existing paper. Choose a fresh conditional width-and-horizon framing from current records; do not inherit a decay-clock thesis from memory.',\n    'staged_input_impact': [\n        'No previous_paper, aggregated, extra_tasks, or edit-context inputs are present; there is no manuscript to preserve, broaden, or recenter.',\n        'Direct inspection of the local selected controller and evaluator establishes one fixed embedding-sum GELU architecture with common hyperparameters, not a new architecture or adaptive training method.',\n        'The final_evaluation block is independently decisive: completed but not_qualified, with five retained width-seed records and four missing records. It changes the framing from an optimization-success narrative to a conditional, incomplete-evidence study.',\n        'downstream_study and study.* grounding sources are absent. Do not invent campaign-level selection margins, scored/unscored counts, or a campaign baseline comparison. Use the configuration explicitly present in final records and the measured fixed-reference arms.'\n    ],\n    'method_groups': [\n        {\n            'name': 'Common-configuration width sweep',\n            'role': 'proposed',\n            'include_reason': 'The studied condition uses learning rate 0.003, AdamW weight decay 1.0, and initialization multiplier 0.5 across widths 32, 128, and 256. Call it the studied configuration, not a novel optimizer or a proven winner. Final evidence exists only for widths 32 and 128.'\n        },\n        {\n            'name': 'Fixed reference',\n            'role': 'baseline',\n            'include_reason': 'Retain the original measured comparison: learning rate 0.001, weight decay 1.0, initialization multiplier 1.0, same width/seed/split/horizon. It differs in both learning rate and initialization, so it is a reference rather than an isolated-component test. Compare only paired retained records and do not assert global validity after failed final qualification.'\n        },\n        {\n            'name': 'Zero-weight-decay control',\n            'role': 'ablation',\n            'include_reason': 'Matches the studied learning rate, initialization, width, and split while setting decay to zero. Separates observed finite-horizon behavior with and without decay; it cannot establish that decay is universally necessary or that the delay is a decay clock.'\n        },\n        {\n            'name': 'Matched-initial-global-norm control',\n            'role': 'ablation',\n            'include_reason': 'Globally rescales the initial parameters to the same-seed width-128 norm. Tests a limited initial-norm alternative, not complete capacity or functional matching. At width 128 this arm duplicates the studied condition, including retained hashes and curves, so it is not an independent replication or an extra event.'\n        }\n    ],\n    'results_focus': [\n        'Lead with a complete final availability table: widths 32/128/256 by seeds 60613/70717/80821; observed records are 3/2/0 respectively. Explicitly disclose failed final qualification in the main text, not just the supplement.',\n        'Compare raw held-out endpoint accuracies across widths and the four arms within each evaluation setting. The studied short-horizon width-32 records have higher endpoint accuracy than widths 128 and 256 despite later fitting; do not turn this into a universal inverse-size law.',\n        'Compare fitting and sustained held-out onset directly, with censor arrows: short-horizon records at 6000 updates versus separately labeled final records at 24000. Final width-128 fitting/onset pairs are 300/17600 and 200/19200 updates; final width-32 fitting occurs at 2600, 2400, and 3000 with no observed held-out onset by 24000.',\n        'Use the fixed reference, zero-decay, and norm-matched arms as within-record controls. Retained final reference and zero-decay arms have no sustained held-out onset; the width-128 norm arm duplicates the studied event, not an additional result.',\n        'Treat the two horizons as separate evidence blocks under one question: data pools and seed sets also change. No same-seed, same-pool horizon intervention or causal claim that longer training reverses a width ranking is available.',\n        'The intended final width-256-minus-width-32 contrast and corresponding interactions are unidentified because width-256 records are absent; final paired_contrasts are null. Missing records are not zero accuracy observations, right-censored trajectories, or measured losses.',\n        'Show every seed value and paired differences where supported. Do not use checkpoints or examples as independent trials. Small-seed descriptive variation is the main uncertainty display; any additional uncertainty calculation must be derived reproducibly from retained data and labeled conditional on availability.'\n    ],\n    'figure_plan': [\n        'Figure 1: early overview of the modular-addition network, width/arm controls, and distinct observed-event/censored/missing evidence states; no speculative circuit or causal mechanism.',\n        'Figure 2: two aligned direct-comparison panels of raw endpoint held-out accuracy by width and four arms, one for short-horizon selection observations and one for final observations; preserve absent cells and separate cohorts.',\n        'Figure 3: two aligned onset-comparison panels for the studied configuration, showing training-fit and held-out-onset markers by width and seed, censoring at the relevant horizon, and missing final records without imputed times.'\n    ],\n    'figure_specs': [\n        {\n            'figure_id': 'study_overview',\n            'required': True,\n            'purpose': 'Make the fixed architecture, intervention scope, and evidence states legible before quantitative results.',\n            'figure_type': 'diagram',\n            'preferred_generator': 'generate-concept-figure',\n            'group_by': 'experimental_condition',\n            'metric': 'none; design and observation-state overview only',\n            'split_by': 'matched design versus evidence interpretation',\n            'preferred_span': 'full_width',\n            'target_width_fraction': 1.0,\n            'layout_rationale': 'The inspected paper.sty is single-column, 5.5in text width and 9in text height. Use a 5.5 by about 1.5in two-block overview at full text width on page 1 or 2; a half-width rendering would make the four arm labels illegible. Allow about 0.22 body page including caption.',\n            'allow_progression': False,\n            'max_panels': 2,\n            'max_panels_per_row': 2,\n            'max_categories_per_panel': 4,\n            'max_series_per_panel': 4,\n            'minimum_rendered_font_pt': 8.5,\n            'source_ids': ['final.evidence.episodes'],\n            'implementation_notes': 'Use the concept-figure workflow downstream, not during strategy generation. Preserve editable draw.io source. Represent widths as design settings, not a sequence of successes; do not draw a causal norm-to-grokking arrow. Regenerate raster output at least 2400px wide, never upscale. Prefer a vector PDF if an authentic vector export is available; otherwise use the high-resolution PNG.'\n        },\n        {\n            'figure_id': 'width_endpoint_comparison',\n            'required': True,\n            'purpose': 'Compare width-dependent endpoint behavior and controls without pooling horizons or hiding incomplete final coverage.',\n            'figure_type': 'grouped_seed_dotplot',\n            'preferred_generator': 'matplotlib',\n            'group_by': 'hidden_width',\n            'metric': 'checkpoint evaluation_accuracy at each setting-specific horizon',\n            'split_by': 'short-horizon selection observations versus final observations',\n            'preferred_span': 'full_width',\n            'target_width_fraction': 1.0,\n            'layout_rationale': 'At 5.5in total width use two side-by-side panels, each with only three width categories and four arm colors. Target about 5.5 by 2.4in and 0.34 body page with caption. Shared y limits, a single four-entry legend, short labels, and raw seed dots avoid unreadable half-width multi-panel figures.',\n            'allow_progression': False,\n            'max_panels': 2,\n            'max_panels_per_row': 2,\n            'max_categories_per_panel': 3,\n            'max_series_per_panel': 4,\n            'minimum_rendered_font_pt': 8.5,\n            'source_ids': ['final.evidence.episodes'],\n            'implementation_notes': 'Selection data come directly from /best_correct/evaluation_evidence/episodes in paper_data.json; final data from /final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes. Do not substitute aggregate metric sources for raw curves. Use small deterministic seed offsets, not invented measurements; connect only genuinely paired values if clarity permits. Keep missing width-256 and missing third width-128 final cells visibly empty/labeled, never at zero. Note the width-128 norm-arm identity. Export reproducible PDF and 300-DPI PNG; LaTeX must include the PDF.'\n        },\n        {\n            'figure_id': 'fit_and_generalization_onsets',\n            'required': True,\n            'purpose': 'Show the fit/generalization separation and distinguish measured delayed events, horizon censoring, and unavailable runs.',\n            'figure_type': 'paired_event_time_dotplot',\n            'preferred_generator': 'matplotlib',\n            'group_by': 'hidden_width_with_seed_offsets',\n            'metric': 'sustained training-fit onset and sustained held-out generalization onset in optimizer updates',\n            'split_by': 'short-horizon selection observations versus final observations',\n            'preferred_span': 'full_width',\n            'target_width_fraction': 1.0,\n            'layout_rationale': 'Use the single-column full text width of 5.5in, two panels with three width categories and two marker meanings, about 2.3in high and 0.32 body page with caption. Explicitly label differing x ranges, 6000 and 24000 updates. Width/seed offsets and a shared two-marker legend remain legible at 8.5pt; do not add all four arm trajectories to this figure.',\n            'allow_progression': False,\n            'max_panels': 2,\n            'max_panels_per_row': 2,\n            'max_categories_per_panel': 3,\n            'max_series_per_panel': 2,\n            'minimum_rendered_font_pt': 8.5,\n            'source_ids': ['final.evidence.episodes'],\n            'implementation_notes': 'This is a direct event-time comparison, not a search trajectory. Read event records and check them against curves and the evaluator definition. Training threshold is 0.99, held-out threshold 0.95, each sustained for three 100-update checkpoints; delayed-event criterion is at least 1000 updates between onsets. At the horizon use an arrow only for a recorded censored trajectory, never for a missing run. Do not draw a finite endpoint for unobserved generalization. Export PDF and 300-DPI PNG and include PDF in LaTeX.'\n        }\n    ],\n    'supplementary_plan': {\n        'purpose': 'Add reproducible definitions, complete retained controls, individual seed records, and evidence limitations after the references without relocating qualification failure or missingness essential to the thesis.',\n        'planned_subsections': [\n            {\n                'heading': 'Exact model, data partition, and intervention definitions',\n                'grounded_content': 'Document the two learned 97-by-width embeddings, their sum plus bias, GELU and linear 97-class readout; parameter count 292w+97; initialization coupling across widths; norm rescaling target; full-batch float32 AdamW with all parameters including biases decayed. Detail disjoint 4704/4705-example selection/final pools and within-pool seeded splits (2822/1882 and 2823/1882 train/evaluation). Use local task/evaluate.py, task/evaluate_final.py and the resource policies as method provenance, not as measured-result source IDs.',\n                'source_ids': ['final.evidence.episodes']\n            },\n            {\n                'heading': 'Complete per-seed comparison records',\n                'grounded_content': 'Tabulate all retained final arm endpoints, training/generalization onsets, censor flags, update counts, initial/final state hashes, and missing cells. Show recorded initial/global norms at matching checkpoints for the limited norm-control interpretation, not as proof of norm mediation. Explicitly flag identical width-128 studied and norm-matched records and never count them as independent observations.',\n                'source_ids': ['final.evidence.episodes']\n            },\n            {\n                'heading': 'Selection sensitivity and incomplete alternate settings',\n                'grounded_content': 'Use /all_programs/0, /all_programs/1, and /all_programs/2 evaluation_evidence/episodes to retain raw observations for initialization multiplier 1.0 versus 0.5 at learning rate 0.003/decay 1.0, and the alternate learning rate 0.006/decay 0.5/multiplier 0.5. Organize by intervention, not search order. The alternate faster setting has an absent width-seed record and failed reported assessment; do not call its missing run a measured loss or a protocol bug. These observations do not establish a matched effective regularization or post-fit decay-clock test. Source IDs below anchor available selection summaries only; raw observations must be grounded separately with the explicit JSON pointers, not attributed to a scalar score.',\n                'source_ids': ['result.top_program_01', 'result.top_program_02']\n            },\n            {\n                'heading': 'Censoring, uncertainty, and evaluation limitations',\n                'grounded_content': 'Explain sustained-onset detection, three-checkpoint support, finite follow-up after fitting, and the normalized capped-delay convention. Show how missing records retain design weight in official aggregate metrics but are not measured zero accuracies. Preserve completed/not-qualified status, unknown gate-specific diagnostics, null final paired contrasts, and the distinction between mechanically recorded controls and comprehensive validity. If intervals are added from existing records, disclose tiny seed counts, missingness, selection conditioning, and the estimand; no survival or significance claim from two observed events.',\n                'source_ids': ['final.qualification.status', 'final.qualification.outcome', 'final.qualification.correct', 'final.evidence.episodes', 'final.metric.mean_final_accuracy', 'final.metric.grokking_incidence', 'final.metric.restricted_mean_delay', 'final.metric.complete_episode_fraction', 'final.metric.control_validity_rate']\n            },\n            {\n                'heading': 'Reproducibility and LLM Usage',\n                'grounded_content': 'List the actual supplied code, environment/version records in task/requirements.txt, recorded seeds, source hashes, CPU setup, and downstream figure/table scripts and commands once implemented. Only give measured runtimes from the supplied records, not fabricated throughput projections or guaranteed full-run timings. Include a separate LLM Usage section with the documented ideation, bounded controller edits, framing, and writing roles; do not claim that an LLM independently executed unrecorded experiments. Preserve anonymity and disclose the literature restriction; never state that a prohibited work was encountered unless an actual encounter is documented.',\n                'source_ids': ['final.evidence.episodes']\n            }\n        ]\n    },\n    'forbidden_headings': [\n        'Search Progression', 'Campaign Phases', 'Milestones', 'First Successful Run',\n        'Experiment A / Experiment B', 'Cycle 1 / Cycle 2 / Cycle 3',\n        'From Baseline to Winner', 'A Universal Width Scaling Law',\n        'Proof of the Decay Clock', 'Validated Superiority'\n    ],\n    'forbidden_phrases': [\n        'we then tried', 'subsequent improvements', 'first valid result', 'next-best',\n        'later variants', 'search winner proves', 'larger models always grok faster',\n        'weight decay is necessary for grokking', 'initial norm explains width effects',\n        'longer training caused the ranking reversal', 'zero incidence means grokking is absent',\n        'missing runs failed to generalize', 'final failure proves inferiority',\n        'final failure proves a protocol defect', 'statistically significant', 'state of the art',\n        'eligible', 'correct and eligible', 'correctness rate', 'worker_01', 'worker_02',\n        'combined_score', 'rubric'\n    ],\n    'notes_for_writer': [\n        'This stage produces a strategy only. Do not draft paper.tex, claims_grounding.json, run new training, generate figures, retrieve literature, or run final-paper validation here.',\n        'VENUE.md selects ICLR 2026, anonymous conference paper, 8-9 body pages excluding references. The inspected supplied paper.sty is single-column with 5.5in textwidth and 9in textheight. full_width therefore means the full single column, not a two-column figure*. There is no distinct narrow conference column to target. Retain selected formatting; the age comments in the supplied style do not independently certify current official-template provenance, which must be checked before final submission without changing geometry to fit content.',\n        'Target about 8.8 body pages: title/abstract/introduction and early overview 1.5; related work 0.75; model, interventions, event estimands, and design 2.0; results including coverage table and two quantitative figures 2.8; discussion, uncertainty, identification limits, and limitations 1.45; conclusion 0.3. These allocations include figures and captions, not extra pages. If the final body is 8 pages, its last page must be at least 75% full; never satisfy the budget with repeated caveats or filler.',\n        'Use thesis-first headings: Introduction; Related Work; Width Comparisons and Event Definitions; Experimental Design and Evidence Coverage; Width-Dependent Fitting and Held-Out Accuracy; Delayed Events and Matched Controls; Discussion and Limitations; Conclusion. Spend available space explaining initialization coupling, comparator confounding, delayed-event definitions, descriptive seed uncertainty, and the missing final contrast rather than search chronology.',\n        'A falsifiable explanation to discuss as a hypothesis, not a discovery: width can change fitting speed separately from the process governing held-out generalization, and initial norm or decay may condition that separation. Earlier fitting without prompt generalization is consistent with that separation; norm collapse, a decay-mediated mechanism, and a universal monotone width effect remain unestablished. These are retrospective tests/interpretations, not preregistered predictions.',\n        'Private-only qualification mapping: final.qualification.status=completed, final.qualification.outcome=not_qualified, final.qualification.correct=false. Visible paper wording must explicitly state that final evaluation completed but did not qualify. Repeat this limitation in abstract, results, discussion, and evidence audit; preserve it during review and revision. Exact failing diagnostics are unavailable. Neither retained metrics nor a publication-ready format upgrades scientific qualification.',\n        'Private-only source map: final.evidence.episodes binds /final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes. Selected short-horizon raw evidence is at /best_correct/evaluation_evidence/episodes, matching /all_programs/1/evaluation_evidence/episodes. result.top_program_01 and result.best_correct_score are selection summary sources, not raw scientific metric substitutes. Candidate-reported diagnostics are labeled unverified; do not use their provenance as a validity certification.',\n        'Private-only campaign guardrail: no downstream_study or study.* sources exist. Do not infer scored/unscored attempt counts or score improvements, or import an unavailable campaign baseline. Compare the actual fixed-reference arms instead. The locally inspected controller configuration and final record configuration agree; do not imply a broader independent source-freeze audit than the supplied evidence supports.',\n        'Final evidence: three retained width32 records, two retained width128 records, no retained width256 records, out of nine planned width-seed cells. The two width128 delayed events are at seeds60613 and70717; the missing width128 cell is seed80821. Separate absent records from recorded right-censored trajectories in text, plots, denominators, and all later revisions.',\n        'Private-only aggregate semantics: final.metric.grokking_incidence=0.2222222222222222 is a nine-cell design-weighted aggregate; it is not an unbiased population event probability. final.metric.mean_final_accuracy=0.48813319164009916 includes failure weighting, not the mean of five recorded endpoint accuracies. final.metric.restricted_mean_delay=0.9458333333333333 is a normalized capped functional, not an observed mean time-to-generalization. Keep these aggregates out of headline figures; if retained in an audit table, define the weighting and do not label absent values measured zero.',\n        'The reference differs from the studied condition in learning rate and initialization. The zero-decay intervention has finite-horizon scope. Initial-global-norm matching does not match capacity, function, representations, or effective regularization; at width128 it is identical to the studied condition. No circuit, lazy-to-rich transition, random-label control, frozen-feature control, width-by-decay grid, or positive-decay clock collapse was measured.',\n        'Report every seed observation and paired contrasts where data exist, with no example/checkpoint pseudoreplication. If reproducible seed-level uncertainty is computed downstream, label it exploratory and conditional on retained records; tiny-sample bootstrap intervals do not prove significance, equivalence, or lack of an effect. Do not silently use 128-minus32 as if it were the planned 256-minus32 contrast.',\n        'Include a compact main-text 3-by-3 final availability table, with separate event/censored/missing states and a clear seed key. Essential missingness and failed qualification cannot be relegated to Supplementary Information. Keep separate reader-facing meanings for training fit (0.99), held-out onset (0.95), and delayed event (onset separation at least1000 updates).',\n        'Use one consistent four-arm palette across quantitative figures, method names rather than source-directory names, and at least8.5pt text at final size. Numerical displays must be generated from actual recorded data by retained scripts. Export quantitative PDF and 300-DPI PNG, include PDF whenever present, and regenerate raster-only figures at least2400px full-width or1200px column-width; never upscale.',\n        'Use exactly one required generate-concept-figure overview, early on page1 or2. Downstream orchestration owns its rendering and any marker. Do not generate it in this stage or silently replace its workflow; record an actual failure before choosing a fallback. No illustrative learning curves or fabricated numeric cartoons.',\n        'Literature scope is restricted to authorized starter originals. references.bib and references_provenance.json contain retrieval metadata, not proof that a paper was read; check identities and consult only allowed original works before citation. Use Power for the phenomenon, Loshchilov/Hutter for AdamW, Liu for norm hypotheses, and permitted Kumar/Varma/Nanda/Davies/Nakkiran originals only where needed as distinct theoretical or architectural comparators. Do not inspect citation neighborhoods, recommendations, or unidentified related-work hits, and do not infer the forbidden paper identity.',\n        'After references insert a page break and Supplementary Information with substantive grounded subsections; include a separate LLM Usage section there. Preserve anonymization and omit author identities, acknowledgements, funding, and self-identifying links. Route authoritative later appendix-ranked analyses to the supplement without retroactively editing this strategy or hiding essential evidence.'\n    ],\n    'failure_reason': ''\n}\n\nmemo = '''# Paper framing strategy\n\n## Decision and provisional title\n\n**Status: ready for a bounded standalone paper plan, not scientifically qualified.**\n**Title:** Width and Delayed Generalization in Finite-Horizon Modular Addition.\n**Format:** \"'`paper_standalone`, using `write-paper-standalone`; ICLR 2026, anonymous, 8–9 body pages excluding references.\n\n**Central question.** How does hidden width relate to the separation between training-set fitting and held-out generalization in a fixed modular-addition network under finite training horizons and matched regularization controls?\n\n**Central thesis.** The retained comparisons show earlier fitting at larger widths without monotone short-horizon generalization, while two longer-horizon width-128 delayed events remain conditional observations rather than qualified evidence of a size law or a decay-clock mechanism.\n\nFinal evaluation **completed but did not qualify**. Ground this in `final.qualification.status`, `final.qualification.outcome`, and `final.qualification.correct`, independently of selection results. The precise failing diagnostics remain unknown. Missing records and limited coverage are observable limitations, not proof of the particular cause of non-qualification. Do not relabel the outcome as either inferiority or a protocol/adaptivity defect. Preserve this disclosure in writing, review, audit, and revision. Publication-format readiness does not upgrade scientific qualification.\n\n## Why this story wins\n\nThe strongest object is one empirical width study with condition-specific evidence blocks, not a new optimization algorithm. The fixed architecture and actual controls permit a concrete separation of fitting behavior, held-out performance, and observability. This supports a substantive negative/conditional paper without requiring a positive search result.\n\nThere is no prior manuscript or enabled edit context, and no `previous_paper/`, `aggregated/`, or `extra_tasks/` input. The contract value `prior_paper_story_status: rejected` rejects the inherited untested mechanism framing; it does not imply that a prior manuscript exists. Inspection of the selected controller and local evaluator confirms a fixed architecture, not an architectural innovation. Current final records, not older memory, determine the thesis.\n\nCandidate framings considered:\n\n| Candidate | Decision | Evidence-based reason |\n|---|---|---|\n| Preserve a post-fit decay-clock story | Reject | The proposed positive-decay grid and clock-collapse test were not executed; no mechanism is identified. |\n| Broaden to a universal size or shared mechanism claim | Reject | Only modulo-97 addition and one architecture are measured; final width-256 records are absent. |\n| Recenter on the latest faster-optimizer setting | Reject | It is incomplete and cannot isolate effective regularization or support final superiority. Recency is not evidence strength. |\n| Conditional width, fitting, and finite-horizon generalization study | Select | Direct width/arm/seed records, two retained delayed events, and explicit censored/missing outcomes support one coherent question. |\n\nThe absence of `downstream_study` and `study.*` sources prevents importing campaign selection margins, scored/unscored counts, or a campaign baseline comparison. Use the configuration explicitly present in final records. Retain the measured fixed-reference arm; do not use an unavailable baseline score as a scientific comparison.\n\n## Scientific object and method groups\n\nThe measured task is ordered-pair addition modulo 97. The architecture has two learned input embeddings, their sum plus hidden bias, GELU, and a linear 97-class output. Hidden widths are 32, 128, and 256; parameter count is `292w+97`. Training is full-batch float32 cross-entropy with AdamW, including decay on biases.\n\nOnly four groups belong in the main comparison:\n\n1. **Studied common configuration:** learning rate 0.003, weight decay 1.0, initialization multiplier 0.5 across widths. This is a configuration, not a claimed new method.\n2. **Fixed reference:** learning rate 0.001, weight decay 1.0, initialization multiplier 1.0. It is paired by width/seed/split/horizon but changes two hyperparameters, so it cannot isolate initialization.\n3. **Zero-decay control:** same studied configuration except zero weight decay. Interpret only within the measured horizon and retained pairs.\n4. **Matched-initial-global-norm control:** globally rescale parameters to the same-seed width-128 initial norm. This does not match capacity, functions, or effective regularization. At width 128 it duplicates the studied arm, including retained initial/final hashes and curves; do not count it as independent replication.\n\nThe alternate initialization and faster-update configurations are supplementary sensitivity records, not obligatory narrative branches. Do not invent random-label, frozen-feature, circuit, or architecture controls.\n\n**Falsifiable explanation, explicitly still a hypothesis:** width may alter training-fit speed separately from the process governing held-out generalization; decay and initialization may condition that separation. Earlier fitting without prompt generalization is compatible with the explanation, but the current evidence does not identify a norm-mediated mechanism, clock collapse, or a universal size relationship. Do not call these predictions preregistered.\n\n## Evidence and results views\n\n### Keep the evidence blocks separate\n\nThe short-horizon studied-configuration records use 6000 updates, seeds 101/211, and a 4704-pair pool. Final records use 24000 updates, seeds 60613/70717/80821, and a disjoint 4705-pair pool. Seeded training fractions are 0.6 within each pool: train/evaluation counts are 2822/1882 and 2823/1882 respectively. Horizon, pool, and seed set change together. Therefore this is not a controlled horizon-extension experiment, and it cannot establish that longer training caused a width-ranking reversal.\n\n**Private-only source map:** the short-horizon raw observations are `paper_data.json` at `/best_correct/evaluation_evidence/episodes`, also `/all_programs/1/evaluation_evidence/episodes`. Final observations are at `/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes`, bound by `final.evidence.episodes`. The selection-summary IDs `result.top_program_01` and `result.best_correct_score` are not substitutes for raw curve grounding. Candidate diagnostics are marked unverified; do not transform that metadata into validity certification.\n\n### Main-text evidence coverage is mandatory\n\nEach retained width-seed record contains all four arms. The table below describes the **studied arm**, not four independent repetitions:\n\n| Final seed | Width 32 | Width 128 | Width 256 |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 60613 | recorded, held-out onset censored | recorded delayed event | missing |\n| 70717 | recorded, held-out onset censored | recorded delayed event | missing |\n| 80821 | recorded, held-out onset censored | missing | missing |\n\nThere are five recorded cells out of nine planned, with two recorded delayed events. Missing cells are neither observed failures to generalize nor right-censored training curves. All final paired-contrast summaries are null. The intended 256-minus-32 final width contrast and its interactions are unidentified; a descriptive 128-minus-32 comparison must not impersonate it.\n\n### Direct scientific comparisons\n\n- **Fitting versus endpoint performance:** the studied short-horizon width-32 records have higher held-out endpoint accuracy than widths 128 and 256, although larger widths fit earlier. This is a measured finite-horizon pattern, not an inverse-size law.\n- **Delayed events:** the two retained final width-128 records have training-fit/held-out-onset steps 300/17600 and 200/19200. The three width-32 records fit at 2600, 2400, and 3000, but have no held-out onset by 24000.\n- **Controls:** retained final fixed-reference and zero-decay arms have no sustained held-out onset. The width-128 norm control reproduces the studied arm by construction. Show arm endpoints directly; neither this identity nor a norm trajectory proves mediation.\n- **Uncertainty:** show every seed, use genuine within-seed pairs, and keep selection versus final cohorts distinct. Two or three seeds do not support robust population claims. Checkpoints and examples are not experimental replicates. Any downstream interval must be reproducibly derived from actual records and labeled conditional on availability and exploratory; do not promise significance or equivalence.\n\nThe evaluator defines fitting at training accuracy at least 0.99 and held-out onset at accuracy at least 0.95, each sustained for three checkpoints spaced 100 updates apart. Onset is the first checkpoint of that triple. A delayed event requires an onset separation of at least 1000 updates. Keep these three meanings distinct. A normalized capped delay of one is a reporting convention when an onset is missing, not a measured delay equal to the horizon.\n\n**Private-only aggregate caution:** `final.metric.grokking_incidence` is 0.2222222222222222 under nine-cell design weighting; `final.metric.mean_final_accuracy` is 0.48813319164009916 with missing/failure weighting; `final.metric.restricted_mean_delay` is 0.9458333333333333 under the capped convention. These are not an unbiased event probability, the mean accuracy of five recorded runs, or a mean observed event time. Keep them out of headline plots. If an audit table retains them, explain the aggregation, rather than plotting absent records as measured zeros.\n\n## Required figures and layout\n\nThe inspected `paper.sty` defines **one column, 5.5 inches of text width and 9 inches of text height**. There is no two-column conference layout: full-width means the full single column; do not use a two-column `figure*` assumption. Each required figure should use the full text width because reducing any of the composites to half width would reduce label readability. The supplied style'\"'s age comments do not independently establish current official-template provenance; verify that separately before final submission without shrinking the layout to fit.\n\n| Figure | Content and intended placement | Size and readability budget |\n|---|---|---|\n| \"'`study_overview` | Page 1 or 2: fixed network, width/arm design, and observed-event/censored/missing states. Use `generate-concept-figure` downstream; no speculative mechanism arrows. | Full width, about 5.5 × 1.5 in; about 0.22 body page including caption. At most 2 panels, 2 per row, 4 categories, 4 series; minimum final text 8.5 pt. |\n| `width_endpoint_comparison` | Direct seed-dot comparison of endpoint held-out accuracy across widths and the four arms. Separate short-horizon and final panels; shared accuracy scale and explicit missing cells. | Full width, about 5.5 × 2.4 in; about 0.34 body page including caption. At most 2 panels, 2 per row, 3 width categories, 4 arm series; minimum text 8.5 pt. |\n| `fit_and_generalization_onsets` | Direct event-time comparison for the studied configuration: fit and held-out onset by width/seed, with censor arrows only for observed trajectories. Separate 6000- and 24000-update panels. | Full width, about 5.5 × 2.3 in; about 0.32 body page including caption. At most 2 panels, 2 per row, 3 width categories, 2 marker series; minimum text 8.5 pt. |\n\nThe third figure addresses dynamics through event times rather than a chronology plot. No training-curve figure is required because the direct onset view supplies the core separation with less visual density. Keep the coverage table in the main text. Keep a single four-arm palette; identify seeds through small fixed offsets or annotations, not additional colors that exceed the series budget. Mark the norm-control identity rather than treating coincident points as independent confirmations.\n\nUse matplotlib for quantitative figures and retain their generation scripts. Export **PDF plus 300-DPI PNG**, and reference the PDF from LaTeX whenever available. The concept figure must retain editable draw.io source; use a genuine vector PDF if available, otherwise regenerate its raster output at least 2400 pixels wide. Any other raster-only full-width/column-width figure must be at least 2400/1200 pixels wide. Never upscale. Exactly one overview uses `generate-concept-figure`; its downstream orchestration and marker must not be bypassed. No figures are generated at this stage.\n\n## Body-page budget and thesis-first structure\n\nTarget approximately **8.8 body pages**, with figure/caption footprints included:\n\n| Material | Pages | Scientific value |\n|---|---:|---|\n| Title, abstract, introduction, early overview | 1.50 | Define width versus fitting/generalization; state conditional contribution and failed final qualification. |\n| Related work | 0.75 | Distinguish permitted norm, circuit, and lazy-to-rich explanations from actual measurements and different architectures. |\n| Model, interventions, event estimands, design | 2.00 | Explain paired initialization, arm confounds, data partitions, thresholds, censoring, and the intended width contrast. |\n| Results and evidence coverage | 2.80 | Two quantitative figures, coverage table, seed-resolved comparisons, controls, and absence of the planned final contrast. |\n| Discussion, uncertainty, identification limits, limitations | 1.45 | Separate descriptive support from mechanistic non-identification; analyze finite follow-up, missingness, and cohort confounding. |\n| Conclusion | 0.30 | State the narrow empirical takeaway and unresolved width/decay questions without upgrading qualification. |\n\nIf the body ends at 8 pages, the final page must be at least 75% full. Do not pad or repeat caveats merely to meet length. Use additional space for exact definitions, controlled-comparison logic, seed-level evidence, and sharpened limits, not extra weak claims. Recommended headings: Introduction; Related Work; Width Comparisons and Event Definitions; Experimental Design and Evidence Coverage; Width-Dependent Fitting and Held-Out Accuracy; Delayed Events and Matched Controls; Discussion and Limitations; Conclusion. These are scientific organization units, not experiment chronology.\n\n## Supplementary Information after references\n\nInsert a page break after the body, references, then a new page and **Supplementary Information**. Main-text qualification failure, missingness, and central comparisons stay in the body.\n\n1. **Exact model, data partition, and intervention definitions.** Extend architecture, coupled initialization, parameter counts, train/evaluation splits, norm rescaling, and optimizer semantics from the local task/resource policies. Measured-source anchor: `final.evidence.episodes`; code paths are method provenance, not newly invented source IDs.\n2. **Complete per-seed comparison records.** All final arm endpoints, events, censor flags, updates, state hashes, and missing cells; limited global-norm readouts where actually recorded. Highlight the width-128 identity. Source: `final.evidence.episodes`.\n3. **Selection sensitivity and incomplete alternate settings.** Organize initialization and faster-update sensitivity by condition, never by iteration. Raw observations are at `/all_programs/0`, `/all_programs/1`, and `/all_programs/2` under `evaluation_evidence/episodes`. Selection-summary anchors `result.top_program_01` and `result.top_program_02` do not stand in for those raw observations. Retain incomplete faster-setting evidence without diagnosing an unreported failure cause or counting its missing record as a measured loss.\n4. **Censoring, uncertainty, and evaluation limitations.** Exact onset/capped-delay rules, all seed values, aggregate failure weighting, unavailable final contrasts, failed qualification, and unknown diagnostics. Sources: `final.evidence.episodes`, `final.qualification.*`, and the applicable `final.metric.*` IDs listed in the JSON. Do not present a tiny-sample interval as mechanistic validation.\n5. **Reproducibility and separate LLM Usage.** Actual dependencies, CPU settings, seeds, hashes, supplied code, and downstream figure/table script mappings and commands once implemented. Runtime claims must come from recorded timings, not resource-budget projections. Disclose documented LLM roles precisely; do not claim unrecorded training or literature consultation. Keep the required LLM Usage section separate and preserve anonymity.\n\nRoute authoritative later appendix-ranked analyses to the supplement without modifying this strategy retroactively. Do not guess such rankings now.\n\n## Literature, language, and audit guardrails\n\nThe bibliography/provenance contain retrieval metadata, not evidence that originals were read. Restrict downstream consultation and citation to exact user-authorized starter originals after identity checks. Prioritize Power for the phenomenon, Loshchilov/Hutter for AdamW, Liu for norm hypotheses; use allowed Kumar, Varma, Nanda, Davies, or Nakkiran originals only when they add a necessary comparator. No browsing is needed for strategy generation. Do not inspect neighboring hits, citation networks, or try to identify the prohibited work. Do not invent an accidental-encounter report.\n\nAvoid headings such as **Search Progression**, **Campaign Phases**, **Milestones**, **First Successful Run**, **Experiment A / Experiment B**, **Cycle 1 / Cycle 2 / Cycle 3**, **From Baseline to Winner**, **A Universal Width Scaling Law**, **Proof of the Decay Clock**, and **Validated Superiority**.\n\nAvoid diary phrases: “we then tried,” “subsequent improvements,” “first valid result,” “next-best,” and “later variants.” Avoid unsupported scientific wording: “search winner proves,” “larger models always grok faster,” “weight decay is necessary for grokking,” “initial norm explains width effects,” “longer training caused the ranking reversal,” “zero incidence means grokking is absent,” “missing runs failed to generalize,” “final failure proves inferiority,” “final failure proves a protocol defect,” “statistically significant,” and “state of the art.”\n\n**Private-only visible-language blacklist:** `eligible`, `correct and eligible`, `correctness rate`, `worker_01`, `worker_02`, `combined_score`, `rubric`. Do not expose these labels in paper prose or plots. Use substantive method names, training-fit onset, held-out generalization onset, and delayed-event counts with explicit denominators. Do not mechanically relabel configuration-level search entries as independent seeded experiments.\n\nThe final submission must omit identifying authors, affiliations, acknowledgements, funding, and self-identifying links. This stage writes neither the final manuscript nor claims mapping, runs no new experiments or final-paper validation, and generates no figures. Strategy-only checks should verify JSON structure, grounding-source existence, data/pointer consistency, figure budgets, and agreement between these two strategy outputs.\n'\"'''\n\nfor filename, content in [('paper_strategy.json', json.dumps(strategy, indent=2, ensure_ascii=False) + '\\\\n'), ('paper_strategy.md', memo)]:\n    print(f'diff --git a/{filename} b/{filename}')\n    print('new file mode 100644')\n    print('--- /dev/null')\n    print(f'+++ b/{filename}')\n    lines = content.splitlines()\n    print(f'@@ -0,0 +1,{len(lines)} @@')\n    for line in lines:\n        print('+' + line)\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
